Claim 10 depends on claim 5 and therefore inherits the underlying analog-to-digital converter, sampling switch, and bootstrap architecture. From the supplied claim text, the inherited subject matter includes:

- an ADC;
- a sampling switch having first and second channel terminals and a control terminal;
- a sampling capacitor coupled between the second channel terminal and ground;
- a first bootstrap module coupled to the control terminal;
- a second bootstrap module coupled between the control terminal and the first channel terminal; and
- operation in track and hold modes, with a sampling instant transitioning the switch from hold mode to track mode.

Claim 10 adds a particular bootstrap-switch arrangement: a switch device is connected between the second terminal of the bootstrap capacitor and ground, and that switch lowers the capacitor’s second-terminal voltage by a given voltage in response to a control signal. This operation must occur when the hold mode is converted into the sample mode.

Paragraph 26 is the best selection because it expressly describes the same switch-mode control sequence and identifies the relevant control terminal. It states that the n-type MOS transistor 251 can be configured into track, hold, and sampling modes by the second clock signal .phi.2. In particular, the transistor is in hold mode when .phi.2 is logic “0,” in track mode when .phi.2 is logic “1,” and has a sampling instant when .phi.2 changes from logic “1” to logic “0.” Thus, it directly addresses the claimed transition from hold mode into sample/track mode and the control signal associated with that transition.

Paragraph 26 does not expressly disclose the claim 10 detail of a switch between the bootstrap capacitor’s second terminal and ground that lowers that terminal by a given voltage. Nor does the supplied excerpt expressly identify the claimed sampling capacitor as the “bootstrap capacitor.” Those remain material distinctions when assessing anticipation, but paragraph 26 is still the most relevant paragraph because it supplies the closest disclosed control behavior for the added claim 10 mode conversion.

Paragraph 44 is the closest competitor because it explains the track-mode operation: capacitor 271 is coupled to the transistor, the gate voltage has a substantially constant difference from the input signal, and a conducting channel forms with substantially constant channel charges. That language is relevant to the inherited bootstrap and track-mode limitations. However, it does not describe the claimed hold-to-sample transition or the switch device that lowers the capacitor’s second terminal. Paragraph 26 is therefore more probative of the specific mode-conversion control feature that distinguishes claim 10.

Paragraph 20 merely identifies the general block diagram and component names, while paragraphs 5 and BRIEF DESCRIPTION OF THE DRAWINGS are only figure-description captions. They provide no comparable operational disclosure of the claimed mode conversion. Accordingly, paragraph 26 is the single most relevant cited paragraph, even though the supplied text does not establish every limitation of claim 10.

